{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"b2cag24kn","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QWXpuNeVfLR4wVPwUcqGWc","status":"completed","output_file":"/tmp/claude-441489/-scratch-users-kushinm-auto-psych-outer-loop-live-runs-run3-repo/d626c3f9-625a-45ba-b188-f058df8d1f12/tasks/b2cag24kn.output","summary":"Background command \"D=/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_1\ncat << 'EOF' > $D/hypothesis.md\nPeople do not weigh the two sequences symmetrically: they read the left sequence first and treat it as the default answer, keeping it unless the second sequence beats it, so the choice depends not only on which sequence looks more random but on how random the pair looks overall. When both sequences look convincingly random (each better explained by their own picture of a fair coin than by a regular generator — a switch-biased coin, a biased coin, or a short repeating motif), people settle for the first one they read; when both look regular, they reject the first and move to the second — so the same sequence is chosen more or less often depending on how random its partner looks.\nEOF\necho first_read_satisficing_anchor > $D/model_name.txt\nsed -n '1,0p' /dev/null\npython3 - << 'PY'\nsrc=open('/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/models/second_order_motif_side_habit.py').read()\ndoc_end=src.index('\"\"\"',3)+3\nbody=src[doc_end:]\ndoc='''\"\"\"First-read satisficing anchor on the pair's overall randomness.\n\nHypothesis: people read the left sequence first and treat it as the default\nanswer. Each sequence's randomness R is the log evidence that the person's own\nfair coin (person-specific believed switch rate, second-order switch belief)\nexplains it better than the regular generators (switch-biased Markov coin,\nbiased coin, repeating motif with slips, person-specific motif suspicion).\nThe choice is driven by the difference R_a - R_b (as in the current best model)\nplus a comparison term kappa * (R_a + R_b): when both sequences look random the\nfirst-read one is kept (kappa > 0), when both look regular people move on to\nthe second. So a sequence's chance of being chosen depends on its partner's\nabsolute randomness, not only on the difference. Person-specific side habits\nremain as in the base model.\n\"\"\"\n'''\nold='''    pid = participant_id\n    log_chance2_diff = 0.5 * d_switch2 * alt2_diff\n    eta = (w[pid] * switch_diff + beta[pid] * (log_chance2_diff - log_regular_diff)) * length_scale\n'''\nnew='''    pid = participant_id\n    log_chance2_diff = 0.5 * d_switch2 * alt2_diff\n    eta = (w[pid] * switch_diff + beta[pid] * (log_chance2_diff - log_regular_diff)) * length_scale\n\n    # Absolute randomness of each sequence (needed for the pair-level term):\n    # log P_chance - log P_regular, with the person's believed switch log-odds\n    # logit_q = w / beta, the second-order shift, and the regular generators'\n    # normalised prior weights (1 : 1 : exp(a_i)).\n    logit_q = w[pid] / beta[pid]\n    log_norm = pt.log(2.0 + pt.exp(a_motif))\n\n    def _randomness(idx, k_vec, alt2_vec):\n        n = seq_len[idx]\n        log_chance = (\n            math.log(0.5)\n            + k_vec * logit_q\n            - (n - 1.0) * pt.softplus(logit_q)\n            + 0.5 * d_switch2 * alt2_vec\n        )\n        return log_chance - (_log_regular(idx) - log_norm)\n\n    r_a = _randomness(idx_a, seq_k[idx_a], seq_alt2[idx_a])\n    r_b = _randomness(idx_b, seq_k[idx_b], seq_alt2[idx_b])\n\n    # First-read satisficing: kappa > 0 keeps the first (left) sequence when the\n    # pair looks random overall and moves on to the second when it looks regular.\n    kappa = pm.Normal(\"kappa\", mu=0.0, sigma=0.5)\n    eta = eta + kappa * 0.5 * (r_a + r_b) * length_scale\n'''\nassert old in body\nbody=body.replace(old,new)\n# add per-sequence k and alt2 tables\nbody=body.replace('''        \"seq_len\": np.asarray([t[\"n\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n''','''        \"seq_len\": np.asarray([t[\"n\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n        \"seq_k\": np.asarray([t[\"k\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n        \"seq_alt2\": np.asarray([t[\"alt2\"] for t in table], dtype=\"float64\"),\n''')\nbody=body.replace('''    seq_len = pm.Data(\"seq_len\", np.full(1, 2.0))\n''','''    seq_len = pm.Data(\"seq_len\", np.full(1, 2.0))\n    seq_k = pm.Data(\"seq_k\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\"))\n    seq_alt2 = pm.Data(\"seq_alt2\", np.zeros(1, dtype=\"float64\"))\n''')\nopen('/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_1/candidate.py','w').write(doc+body)\nPY\ncd /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/runs/run3/repo && /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/venv/bin/python -m src.pipelines.inner_loop.check_candidate --candidate-dir $D --responses /scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run3/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/responses.csv 2>&1 | tail -5\" completed (exit code 0)","uuid":"951020f3-cd41-472d-a293-ab5d2dc1b42b","session_id":"d626c3f9-625a-45ba-b188-f058df8d1f12"}
