{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfjEAMVqE38f9PwhABNkY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the candidate `first_read_satisficing_anchor` and it passes the self-check (`OK`). The 100-draw smoke fit's ELPD-LOO was −2103.3, about level with the current best model (−2103.4).\n\n**Hypothesis:** People read the left sequence first and treat it as the default answer. When both sequences look convincingly random, they keep the first one. When both look regular, they reject it and switch to the second. So the same sequence is chosen more or less often depending on how random its partner looks, which is the pair-dependent comparison the brief asked for.\n\n**Model:** It keeps the best model's per-sequence randomness score unchanged. That score asks how much better each person's own picture of a fair coin explains the sequence than the regular generators do (a switch-biased coin, a biased coin, a short repeating motif). The one addition is a term `kappa · (R_left + R_right)/2`, scaled by length like the rest of the evidence. To compute it, each sequence's score is worked out in full, including the person's believed switch rate, rather than only as the left-minus-right difference. When `kappa = 0` the model is exactly the best model, so the fit decides whether the effect exists. Left/right placement was randomised, so `kappa` can be estimated from the data.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- **It may be rejected as a near-duplicate.** If the fit puts `kappa` near 0, the model will predict almost exactly like the best model and fail the novelty gate.\n- **It doesn't address the critique.** The one critique that survived the correction for multiple tests (people choose the head-heavier sequence less often than predicted) isn't something this mechanism can explain. I put the brief's comparison mechanism first.\n- **Convergence is unchecked.** The smoke fit ran a single chain, so it couldn't report R-hat, and it warned